
Predicting Diamond Prices with caret
========================================================
author: Arun
date: `r format(Sys.Date(), "%B %d, %Y")`
autosize: true

Why Caret & Diamond Modeling?
========================================================

Diamond retail pricing often lacks transparency for prospective buyers.

- **Objective:** Provide instant, machine-learning-driven price appraisals based on physical diamond metrics.
- **Key Inputs:**
  - Carat weight (continuous slider)
  - Cut quality (ordered factor selection)
  - Clarity grading (categorical dropdown)
- **Engine:** Trained using the `caret` regression pipeline in R.

Training Data: ggplot2::diamonds
========================================================

The underlying algorithm evaluates empirical diamond sales data:

```{r load-data, echo=TRUE}
library(ggplot2)
data(diamonds)
str(diamonds[, c("carat", "cut", "clarity", "price")])
```



Caret Model Training & Performance
========================================================
The application uses caret::train to fit an ordinary least squares model:

library(caret)

set.seed(123)

fit <- train(price ~ carat + cut + clarity, data = diamonds[1:1000, ], method = "lm")

fit$results[, c("RMSE", "Rsquared", "MAE")]

========================================================
Application Architecture & Links:

Reactive Pipeline: User selections trigger dynamic predictions through the trained caret object.

Embedded Instructions: Guidance documentation is placed directly on the sidebar for novice users.

